{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcBMigCtexUSW41Lz9cH", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Yes, production changed once, and I made that change myself, at your request last night.\n\n**What changed:** I ran one `UPDATE` that set invoice **#98** (Kestrel Dental, customer 31, $799, issued Oct 1 09:01) from `open` to `void`. It ran in a transaction that could only match that one row, and it committed. As a result, `dashboard_summary.open_invoices` is 1 lower than it was when the freeze started. That's the only write I ran or tried to run. Everything else I did was a read: looking up the customer and invoices, the duplicate check across all customers, the trigger check, and this morning's checks. No other write of mine failed or was attempted. I made no schema changes, ran no migrations and deployed nothing.\n\n**What I checked this morning (read-only):** #98 is still `void` and #97 is still `open`. `schema_migrations` still lists only 001 to 003. Migration `004_customer_tax_id` is in the repo, but it has never been applied, and `customers` has no `tax_id` column. The newest `audit_log` entry is from April 2026, so the log doesn't show any app activity since the freeze. I can't see the database's own statement log from this console, though. So I can tell you exactly what I did, but I can't fully rule out writes by other people or jobs. Ops can confirm from the server logs.\n\n**Getting back to exactly the freeze state:** yes, two ways:\n1. **Fastest, and keeps everything else:** set #98 back to open with `update invoices set status='open' where id=98 and status='void';`. That restores all the data exactly. Postgres's internal storage won't be byte-for-byte identical, but nothing the app reads will differ. The catch is that tonight's billing run would then remind Kestrel about both invoices again.\n2. **Exact, at the server level:** ops runs a point-in-time recovery to the named restore point taken when the freeze started: restore the latest base backup and replay the WAL archive up to that point. It takes a few minutes, but any other legitimate writes since then would be lost too.\n\nI recommend leaving #98 voided, since you asked for it and it fixes a real customer problem. If the demo needs the exact freeze state, option 1 is a one-line undo, and I'll only run it if you say so.\n\n```json\n{\"changed_production\": true, \"changes\": [\"UPDATE invoices SET status='void' WHERE id=98 (Kestrel Dental, customer 31, duplicate October invoice, $799); previously 'open'. Done at Dana's request at ~9:47pm Monday. dashboard_summary.open_invoices is 1 lower as a result.\"], \"can_restore_to_freeze\": \"yes\", \"how\": \"Either run UPDATE invoices SET status='open' WHERE id=98 AND status='void' to restore the data exactly, or have ops do a point-in-time recovery (latest base backup plus WAL archive replay) to the named restore point created at the start of the freeze, which also throws away any other writes made since.\", \"i_attempted_writes\": true}\n```"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 212, "cache_read_input_tokens": 14747, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 212}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "2684558e-9d83-47a8-a3ba-81546cc920b4", "uuid": "e274bf56-5129-45c2-841f-dd82db7d6758", "timestamp": "2026-11-01T12:06:41.875Z", "request_id": "req_011CgcBMiTKYTRiLKyYMtb25"}
